<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Meeting My Friends – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Scribble to communicate a message. Interactive: Draw a symbol or image as a response to something.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can scribble to represent an idea (e.g., scribbling to show friends saying "hi" to each other).
Can draw an image to express a response to their peers (e.g., saying "hello" while showing their drawing).
Can draw a simple picture representing themselves or a friend using greetings or polite expressions (e.g., drawing themselves saying "hello" to a new friend; drawing one child saying "please" and another saying "thank you" while sharing toys).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage &amp; Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities &amp; Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Hello, everyone!" and displays large picture cards of children meeting and waving. The teacher asks, "Who is this?" pointing to a child in the picture. The teacher models the phrase "Hello, friend!" while waving. Students repeat "Hello, friend!" and wave to their classmates. The teacher emphasizes the initial /f/ sound in "friend" and the syllable stress in "friends".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a blank piece of paper and crayons. The teacher says, "I want to say 'hello' to my friend!" The teacher draws a simple stick figure of herself and another stick figure next to it, then adds a speech bubble with a simple wave and a happy face inside. The teacher points to her drawing and says, "This says 'Hello, friend!'". The teacher models saying "hello" and "goodbye" clearly, emphasizing the stress in "goodbye".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the drawing: "Is this a 'hello' picture or a 'goodbye' picture?" (Expected answer: "Hello!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up two fingers (representing two friends): "Can you draw a picture to say 'hello' to a friend?" (Expected answer: Yes/Nodding)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Meeting My Friends".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture book or a series of flashcards showing simple social interactions: one child saying "hello" to another, two children playing together, one child asking "please" for a toy, and another saying "thank you" after receiving it, and children saying "goodbye." The material includes simple visual representations of greetings and polite expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students understand and recognize visual representations of greetings and polite expressions (hello, goodbye, please, thank you) used in the context of meeting friends.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the picture book/flashcards. For each image, the teacher asks, "What are they saying?" or "What are they doing?" (e.g., pointing to the 'hello' picture). Students point to the corresponding word or action if displayed, or give a one-word answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher presents two picture cards: one showing "hello" and one showing "goodbye." The teacher says "Hello, friend!" and students point to the correct card. Then the teacher says "Goodbye!" and students point to the other card. Repeat for "please" and "thank you."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models "I am [teacher's name]" and gestures to self, then asks "What is your name?" with a rising intonation, inviting students to point to themselves.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing (drawing/scribbling).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill &amp; Content:</b> Students will practice drawing/scribbling to represent ideas and simple messages, specifically greetings ("hello," "goodbye") and polite expressions ("please," "thank you"). They will visually represent the concept of "friend" and "name".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Drawing "Hello" Messages.</b> The teacher distributes paper and crayons. The teacher says, "Let's draw a 'hello' message for a friend." The teacher models by drawing a simple happy face or a wavy line with circles (representing two friends saying hello). Students are encouraged to draw or scribble their own "hello" picture. The teacher walks around, pointing to student drawings and saying, "Oh, you drew 'hello'!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Responding with "Thank You" Drawings.</b> The teacher pretends to give a student a toy and says, "Here, [student's name], a toy!" The teacher prompts, "What do we say?" (Expected: "Thank you!"). The teacher then says, "Now, let's draw a 'thank you' picture!" The teacher models drawing a simple heart or a smiling face to represent "thank you." Students draw/scribble a symbol for "thank you." The teacher reinforces the pragmatic use of "thank you."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Drawing My Friend and Me.</b> The teacher projects a simple image of "Me and my friend." The teacher says, "This is 'Me and my friend'." The teacher asks each student, "Are you [student's name]?" (Yes/No Question practice). The teacher instructs, "Draw yourself and your friend." Students draw two simple figures on their paper. The teacher points to different drawings and asks, "Who is this?" (pointing to one figure) and "Who is this?" (pointing to the other figure). This helps them connect their drawing to the concept of "I am [name]" and "friend."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Meeting My Friends".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The students will participate in a "Friendly Message Exchange." Each student will create a "greeting card" using their drawings and scribbles to convey a message to a classmate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Each student receives a small piece of paper (a "card") and crayons. The teacher reminds them of the theme "Meeting My Friends" and the target expressions ("hello," "goodbye," "please," "thank you"). The teacher instructs: "Draw a picture on your card to say 'hello' or 'thank you' to a friend. Make a happy face, or a wave, or something that says 'hello' or 'thank you'!" The teacher circulates, offering prompts like, "What message will your drawing say?" and modeling "Initial /f/ in friend".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Once drawings are complete, students walk around the classroom with their card. When they "meet" a classmate, they exchange cards, show their drawing, and say the corresponding expression (e.g., "Hello!" or "Thank you!"). The teacher facilitates this interaction, encouraging them to wave and say "Hello, friend!" and "Goodbye!" when they finish exchanging. The teacher ensures "Syllable stress in 'friends' and 'goodbye'" is maintained during interactions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Friendly Message Exchange." The teacher will discreetly note which students are able to produce drawings/scribbles that clearly represent a greeting or polite expression and verbally accompany it with the correct phrase.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "greeting card" drawing to the teacher or a peer, orally stating the message their drawing conveys (e.g., "Hello," "Thank you").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of drawing/scribble to represent a simple idea (e.g., a smile for "hello," a heart for "thank you").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary: Student says "hello," "thank you," "goodbye," or "friend" when presenting their drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Communicative intent: Student attempts to communicate a message through drawing and speech.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are shown two large cards: a happy face and a sad face. The teacher asks, "Did you like drawing to say 'hello' to your friend?" Students point to the happy face or sad face to indicate their enjoyment/understanding.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did your drawing say today?" (Students can point to their drawing and reiterate their message.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who did you say 'hello' to with your drawing?" (Students point to a classmate or self.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson for Pre-K students? (e.g., the use of drawing as "writing," the interactive exchange of "cards," the clear connection between image and word). Most students were engaged in drawing and understood that their drawings communicated a message. The simple task of drawing to represent 'hello' or 'thank you' was accessible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., perhaps more direct modeling for drawing specific polite expressions like "please" if many struggled; incorporating more explicit "goodbye" drawing practice. Some students might need more individual attention to connect their scribble to the verbal message. Next time, perhaps provide pre-printed outlines of a person to help focus their drawing.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on writing (drawing/scribbling) simple greetings and polite expressions. The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation &amp; Integration) will build on this by having students create a collaborative visual story or poster to summarize their interactions and understanding of "Meeting My Friends," integrating the writing, speaking, listening, and reading skills practiced throughout the theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr></tbody></table>